WASTEPAY - BARCODE AUDIT WORKFLOW


1. PURPOSE

The Barcode Audit Workflow exists to build WastePay's Plastic Intelligence Database by identifying, classifying, and tracking plastic products recovered across Cameroon and, eventually, across Africa.

The system enables WastePay to:

- Identify plastic products by barcode.
- Track brand and manufacturer plastic footprints.
- Generate environmental impact analytics.
- Support public transparency and corporate accountability.
- Maintain a clean and verified master product catalog.


--------------------------------------------------


2. PRODUCT INFORMATION DATA MODEL

For every verified product in the WastePay Master Barcode Catalog, the following information must be stored:

- Barcode Number
- Product Name
- Brand Family Name
- Parent Manufacturer Corporation
- Product Volume / Size
- Plastic Material Category
- Product Category
- Country of Manufacture

Examples:

- Tangui Mineral Water
- Tangui
- SABC
- 1.5 Liters
- PET
- Water
- Cameroon


--------------------------------------------------


3. PRODUCT HIERARCHY MODEL

Every product follows the following hierarchy:

Manufacturer

↓

Brand Family

↓

Product Name

↓

Product Size

↓

Plastic Material Category

↓

Barcode Number


This hierarchy allows WastePay to accurately calculate corporate plastic footprints and brand-level environmental statistics.


--------------------------------------------------


4. MASTER BARCODE CATALOG

The WastePay Master Barcode Catalog serves as the single source of truth for all plastic products processed by the platform.

Business Rules:

- Depot Agents cannot edit the master catalog.
- Depot Managers cannot edit the master catalog.
- Only the WastePay Super Administrator may permanently verify or archive products.
- Existing products are reused across all future transactions.


--------------------------------------------------


5. UNKNOWN BARCODE PROCESSING PIPELINE

Step 1 - Ground Zero Flagging

If a barcode is not recognised by the system:

- The Depot Agent manually enters a temporary product name.
- The Depot Agent assigns the plastic material type.
- The transaction continues normally.
- The collector is paid normally.
- The barcode status becomes UNVERIFIED.


Step 2 - Depot Verification

The Depot Manager reviews:

- Product information
- Transaction photographs
- Plastic material classification

The barcode status becomes:

PROVISIONALLY APPROVED


Step 3 - Master Catalog Verification

The WastePay Super Administrator reviews the submission and permanently approves or rejects the product.

The barcode status becomes:

VERIFIED ACTIVE


--------------------------------------------------


6. PRODUCT STATUS LIFECYCLE

Every product progresses through the following lifecycle:


NEW BARCODE DETECTED

↓

UNVERIFIED

↓

PROVISIONALLY APPROVED

↓

VERIFIED ACTIVE

↓

ARCHIVED


Business Rule:

Historical product records are never deleted from the database.

Archived products remain available for historical analytics and reporting purposes.


--------------------------------------------------


7. DUPLICATE SCAN MANAGEMENT

If an existing barcode is scanned:

- The system automatically reuses the existing product record.
- No duplicate product records are created.
- A new transaction record is created instead.

Business Rule:

One barcode must correspond to only one product record in the Master Barcode Catalog.


--------------------------------------------------


8. PRODUCT CATEGORY CLASSIFICATION

Products are classified into categories such as:

- Water
- Soft Drinks
- Juice
- Cooking Oil
- Detergent
- Shampoo
- Cosmetics
- Pharmaceuticals
- Household Cleaning Products
- Food Packaging

Business Rule:

Product categories are used for environmental analytics and public reporting.


--------------------------------------------------


9. BARCODE DEDUPLICATION RULES

The system must prevent:

- Duplicate product records.
- Duplicate barcode assignments.
- Duplicate manufacturer relationships.

Business Rule:

Barcode records are globally unique across the WastePay ecosystem.


